    "id": "pie_electricity_nz_germany",
    "type": "pie",
    "title": "Electricity Generation in New Zealand and Germany",
    "guide": "<strong>Dạng bài:</strong> Pie Chart (Timeline).<br><strong>Gợi ý:</strong><br>- Overview: Cả hai nước đều tăng gấp đôi sản lượng. NZ phụ thuộc hoàn toàn vào Than vào 2010. Germany phụ thuộc vào Hạt nhân vào 2010.<br>- Body 1: New Zealand (sự lên ngôi của Than từ 56 lên 150 đơn vị).<br>- Body 2: Germany (sự lên ngôi của Hạt nhân từ không có gì lên 155 đơn vị).",
    "barem": "Introduction: Paraphrase.\nOverview: Production doubling; total shift in fuel reliance.\nBody 1: NZ (Coal dominance 75% share by 2010; Hydro 20%).\nBody 2: Germany (Nuclear emergence 75% share; Coal/Petroleum minor).\nConclusion: Summary.",
    "vocab": [
      { "word": "Principal fuel source", "meaning": "Nguồn nhiên liệu chính" },
      { "word": "Enrolment", "meaning": "Sự đăng ký/nhập học" },
      { "word": "Remainder", "meaning": "Phần còn lại" },
      { "word": "Significant source", "meaning": "Nguồn đáng kể" }
    ],
    "modelAnswer": "The pie charts below show electricity generation by source in New Zealand and \n\nGermany in 1980 and 2010. \n\n31 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\fThe charts compare the sources of electricity in New Zealand and Germany in the years 1980 and \n\n2010. Between these years, electricity generation almost doubled, rising from 127 units to 200 in New \n\nZealand, and from 107 to 214 units in Germany. \n\nIn 1980 New Zealand used coal as the main electricity source (56 units) and the remainder was \n\nproduced from natural gas, hydro power (each producing 30 units) and petroleum (which produced \n\nonly 11 units). By 2010, coal had become the fuel for more than 75% of electricity produced and only \n\nhydro continued to be another significant source supplying approximately 20%. \n\nIn contrast, Germany used coal as a source for only 28 units of electricity in 1980, which was matched \n\nby natural gas. The remaining 49 units were produced largely from petroleum and nuclear power, with \n\nhydro contributing only 7 units. But by 2010 nuclear power, which was not used at all in New Zealand, \n\nhad developed into the main source, producing almost 75% of electricity, at 155 units, while coal and \n\npetroleum together produced only 55 units. Other sources were no longer significant. \n\nOverall, it is clear by 2010 these two countries relied on different principal fuel sources: New Zealand \n\nrelied on coal and Germany on nuclear power.",
    "modelAnswerVi": "",
    "modelAnswerEn": "The pie charts below show electricity generation by source in New Zealand and \n\nGermany in 1980 and 2010. \n\n31 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\fThe charts compare the sources of electricity in New Zealand and Germany in the years 1980 and \n\n2010. Between these years, electricity generation almost doubled, rising from 127 units to 200 in New \n\nZealand, and from 107 to 214 units in Germany. \n\nIn 1980 New Zealand used coal as the main electricity source (56 units) and the remainder was \n\nproduced from natural gas, hydro power (each producing 30 units) and petroleum (which produced \n\nonly 11 units). By 2010, coal had become the fuel for more than 75% of electricity produced and only \n\nhydro continued to be another significant source supplying approximately 20%. \n\nIn contrast, Germany used coal as a source for only 28 units of electricity in 1980, which was matched \n\nby natural gas. The remaining 49 units were produced largely from petroleum and nuclear power, with \n\nhydro contributing only 7 units. But by 2010 nuclear power, which was not used at all in New Zealand, \n\nhad developed into the main source, producing almost 75% of electricity, at 155 units, while coal and \n\npetroleum together produced only 55 units. Other sources were no longer significant. \n\nOverall, it is clear by 2010 these two countries relied on different principal fuel sources: New Zealand \n\nrelied on coal and Germany on nuclear power."
