    "id": "pie_energy_sources_usa",
    "type": "pie",
    "title": "Main Energy Sources in the USA",
    "guide": "<strong>Dạng bài:</strong> Pie Chart (Timeline).<br><strong>Gợi ý:</strong><br>- Overview: Dầu (Oil) vẫn là nguồn chính nhưng giảm tỷ trọng. Năng lượng hạt nhân (Nuclear) tăng gấp đôi.<br>- Body 1: Oil (giảm từ 42% xuống 33%) và Coal (tăng lên 27%).<br>- Body 2: Natural gas và Hydroelectric (ổn định hoặc giảm nhẹ).",
    "barem": "Introduction: Paraphrase.\nOverview: Oil leads but declines; Nuclear power doubles its share.\nBody 1: Fossil fuels (Oil drop, Coal rise, Gas stable).\nBody 2: Nuclear (10% growth) and Hydro (5% constant).\nConclusion: Summary.",
    "vocab": [
      { "word": "Major energy source", "meaning": "Nguồn năng lượng chính" },
      { "word": "Throughtout the decade", "meaning": "Trong suốt thập kỷ" },
      { "word": "Market share", "meaning": "Thị phần/Tỷ trọng thị trường" },
      { "word": "Radically", "meaning": "Một cách triệt để/hoàn toàn" }
    ],
    "modelAnswer": "The two graphs show the main sources of energy in the USA in the 1980s and the 1990s. \n\nThe two graphs show that oil was the major energy source in the USA in both 1980 and 1990 and that \n\ncoal, natural gas and hydroelectric power remained in much the same proportions. On the other hand, \n\nthere was a dramatic rise in nuclear power, which doubled its percentage over the ten years. \n\nOil supplied the largest percentage of energy, although the percentage decreased from 42% in 1980 \n\nto 33% in 1990. Coal in 1990 was the second largest source of energy, increasing its proportion to \n\n27% from 22% in the previous decade. Natural gas, the second largest source in 1980 at 26%, \n\ndecreased its share very slightly to provide 25% of America’s energy ten years later. There was no \n\nchange in the percentage supplied by hydroelectric power which remained at 5% of the total energy \n\nused. Nuclear power the greatest change: in 1990 it was 10%, twice that of the 1980s.",
    "modelAnswerVi": "",
    "modelAnswerEn": "The two graphs show the main sources of energy in the USA in the 1980s and the 1990s. \n\nThe two graphs show that oil was the major energy source in the USA in both 1980 and 1990 and that \n\ncoal, natural gas and hydroelectric power remained in much the same proportions. On the other hand, \n\nthere was a dramatic rise in nuclear power, which doubled its percentage over the ten years. \n\nOil supplied the largest percentage of energy, although the percentage decreased from 42% in 1980 \n\nto 33% in 1990. Coal in 1990 was the second largest source of energy, increasing its proportion to \n\n27% from 22% in the previous decade. Natural gas, the second largest source in 1980 at 26%, \n\ndecreased its share very slightly to provide 25% of America’s energy ten years later. There was no \n\nchange in the percentage supplied by hydroelectric power which remained at 5% of the total energy \n\nused. Nuclear power the greatest change: in 1990 it was 10%, twice that of the 1980s."
